    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d339">

                <pb facs="0796" n="754" sameAs="frus:frus1951v04p1/pg754"/>

                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">No. 339</seg>
                </opener>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Editorial Note</hi>
                </head>
                <p>For documentation on the Netherlands defense effort and attitude toward a German
                    contribution to the defense of Western Europe, see <ref
                        target="frus:frus1951v03p1/pg_1">volume <hi rendition="#small-caps"
                        >iii</hi>, Part 1, pages 1</ref> ff. and <ref
                        target="frus:frus1951v03p1/pg_990">990</ref> ff. For documentation on the
                    interest of the Netherlands in the economic and political integration of Western
                    Europe, see Documents 1 ff. herein. For documentation on the interest of the
                    Netherlands in the control of exports to Eastern Europe and the Soviet Union,
                    see volume I, pages 993 ff. An exchange of correspondence between Queen Juliana
                    and President Truman on the problems of
                    refugee relief in Europe is printed in Department of State <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Bulletin</hi>, October 8 and 29, 1951, pages 572 and
                    701. For documentation regarding United States policy on the question of
                    European tariff disparities, raised by the Netherlands in the Torquay tariff
                    negotiating conference, 1950–1951, see volume I, pages 319 ff.</p>
            </div>
